contentText:
Mötley Crüe &#8216;s Tommy Lee is opening up about one of the darkest parts of his history.

Appearing on the Wednesday (July 22) episode of Armchair Expert With Dax Shepard , the drummer looked back on his struggles with heroin addiction during the band&#8217;s late-&#8217;80s heyday, recalling the moment he realized how dangerous his substance use had become.

Lee remembered running out of heroin while spending the night with his &#8220;bass player&#8221; &#8212; the rocker doesn&#8217;t name the band&#8217;s bassist, Nikki Sixx &#8212; and how the pair decided to inject Jack Daniel&#8217;s whiskey.

&#8220;&#8216;What are we gonna do,'&#8221; he recalled thinking after running out of drugs around 3 or 4 a.m. The rocker then told Shepard how the two men unscrewed the cap off a fifth of Jack, filled the cap with some alcohol, then filled a few syringes with the whiskey. &#8220;We were shooting Jack Daniels.&#8221;

&#8220;It was at that moment where I went, &#8216;OK! We could just drink this, but we&#8217;re shooting it,'&#8221; Lee reflected. &#8220;&#8216;This is f&#8211;king wrong, and somebody&#8217;s gonna die.&#8221;

The members of Mötley Crüe entered rehab and group therapy in the late 1980s as they prepared to record their fifth studio album, 1989&#8217;s Dr. Feelgood , which hit No. 1 on the Billboard 200 and remained on the list for 109 weeks. &#8220;We all decided we needed to go to rehab or someone was going to die,&#8221; Lee said. &#8220;People were eating handfuls of [sleeping pills] and then go out for the night. We were zombies and people weren&#8217;t waking up. We&#8217;re like, &#8216;Someone&#8217;s going to die soon.'&#8221; Looking back on that period, Lee said that the band relocated to Vancouver, B.C., in order to make the album while working on staying sober together.

&#8220;We&#8217;d all get together and talk about how everybody&#8217;s dealing with this new arrangement,&#8221; he said. &#8220;Once you start getting into therapy with the people that you work with and that are also going through the same thing, you&#8217;re learning all these other things about them, and all of a sudden it&#8217;s becoming really complicated at the same time.&#8221;

Lee also acknowledged that sobriety has remained an ongoing journey. He told Shepard: &#8220;I think three years is the longest I&#8217;ve done.&#8221;

The conversation adds another chapter to the band&#8217;s well-documented history with addiction, which Sixx discussed in his 2007 memoir, The Heroin Diaries .

